WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Chapter three. From answering to doing.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.440
Right. This is the one that changes how you

3
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.960
think about everything else.

4
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.160
For about two years these tools did a single

5
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.120
thing. You asked a question,

6
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.840
an answer came back,

7
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.340
and then you went off and did the actual work

8
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.980
yourself. Genuinely useful.

9
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.560
Also a very fast writer with no hands.

10
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.240
What changed is that the model stopped answering

11
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.800
and started acting.

12
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.540
Here is what that looks like in practice.

13
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.260
You give it an outcome.

14
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.500
You give it the bounds it has to stay inside.

15
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.480
Then it works out the route itself.

16
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.160
It uses tools, it looks at what came back,

17
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.440
and when that turns out to be wrong it goes

18
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.640
again. It keeps going until the thing holds,

19
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.480
or until it gets stuck and says so.

20
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.420
Four things have to be true before any of

21
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.260
that works. It has to be chasing an outcome.

22
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.240
A list of steps will not do it.

23
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.120
It has to be allowed to use real tools.

24
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:08.880
There has to be a loop,

25
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.180
so it sees the consequence of its own last

26
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.180
move. And it has to remember what it already

27
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.120
tried. Take one of those away and you are

28
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.040
back to a chatbot with extra steps.

29
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.860
The useful way to picture this is a staff

30
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.540
officer. Capable, quick,

31
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.300
tireless, widely read,

32
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.640
and completely dependent on you for direction.

33
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.380
Which makes your job the one you already know

34
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.000
how to do. Set the intent.

35
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.520
Set the bounds. Then check the work before

36
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.060
it goes anywhere near anyone else.

37
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.320
That last part is now most of the job.

38
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.800
And here is the bit worth sitting with for

39
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.320
a second. Someone who is out of their depth

40
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.660
hesitates. They hedge,

41
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:45.900
they come back with questions,

42
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.160
and you read all of that as a warning long

43
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.620
before you read their actual work.

44
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.900
An agent that is wrong reads exactly like

45
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.760
an agent that is right.

46
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.940
Same fluency, same confidence,

47
00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.460
same speed. So the signals you have spent

48
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.720
a career learning to read simply are not available

49
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.380
here. Which leaves one thing.

50
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:03.080
You check it yourself,

51
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.160
every single time.

